    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d830">

                <pb facs="0911" n="1745" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg1745"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 652.119/1916</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Consul at Curaçao</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Messersmith</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-10-19T17:00:00-04:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">October 19, 1918, 5 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Your October 8, 11 a.m. We have transmitted the following cable to the American
                    Consul at Puerto Cabello: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>War Trade Board requires Spanish steamer <hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Villamanrique</hi> to call at Porto Rico after leaving Venezuela
                                <hi rendition="#italic">en route</hi> to Canaries and Spain.</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>[The War Trade Board, in considering the plan proposed on October 16 for
                        examining neutral vessels proceeding across the Atlantic to or from northern
                        neutral countries, considered also a plan for examining in a port of Porto
                        Rico or of the United States, Spanish vessels proceeding to or from Spain.
                        These plans, however, were not approved by the Department of State. See <hi
                            rendition="#italic">ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg1003">pages 1003</ref>,
                            <ref target="#pg1010">1010</ref>, and <ref target="#pg1012"
                        >1012</ref>.]</p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
